  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;be the centre of the circle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math>&nbsp;be a point on the circle. A line passes through the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>, makes an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></math> with the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>P</mi></math> and intersects the circle at the points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. Then the area of the triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi><mi>R</mi></math> (in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>unit</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>) is",
    "image": null
  },
